# {{.AppName}}

A Go + React app generated by [gor](https://github.com/gor). Go API (Echo + pgx + sqlc),
React SPA (Vite + TypeScript + TanStack Query), one production binary.

## Development

```bash
gor db setup   # start Postgres via Docker and run migrations
gor dev        # Go API on :8080, Vite on :5173 (proxies /api)
```

Open http://localhost:5173. The Vite dev server proxies `/api` to the Go server,
so there are no CORS issues in development.

Without Docker: start Postgres yourself, put its URL in `.env`
(see `.env.example`), then `gor db migrate`.

> Note: migrations use `uuidv7()`, which needs Postgres 18+. On older
> Postgres, switch the `id` defaults to `gen_random_uuid()`.

## Generate a resource

```bash
gor g resource post title:string body:text published:bool
```

This creates the migration, sqlc queries, REST handlers under
`internal/post/`, a typed API client, and React pages under
`web/src/features/posts/` — then wires up routes on both sides.

Run `gor db migrate` after generating.

## Change the schema later

```bash
gor g migration add_slug_to_posts slug:string   # writes the ALTER TABLE
gor db migrate                                  # apply it
```

The migration also regenerates the queries, handler, API client, schema, and
pages for that resource (tracked in `.gor/manifest.json`). Files you've
edited by hand are skipped and listed so you can update them yourself;
`gor g sync post` re-runs the regeneration on demand.

Seed dev data with `gor db seed` (edit `cmd/seed/main.go`). File uploads:
`gor g uploads`, then give resources an `attachment:file` field.

## Authentication

```bash
gor g auth                                       # users, sessions, middleware, login UI
gor db migrate
gor g resource note title:string --protected     # resource behind auth.Require
```

Role-gate backend routes with `auth.RequireRole("admin")`, frontend routes
with `<RequireRole role="admin" />`. Promote an admin:
`UPDATE users SET role = 'admin' WHERE email = '...';`

`gor g auth-emails` adds password reset + email verification (pages at
/forgot-password, /reset-password, /verify-email).

## Background jobs

```bash
gor g jobs                      # once: River setup + worker process
gor g job send_welcome_email    # generate a job
gor g job cleanup --every 1h    # scheduled job (runs each hour + at startup)
gor g job report --cron "0 9 * * *"  # at specific times (daily 9am)
```

Enqueue with `jobs.Enqueue(ctx, jobs.SendWelcomeEmailArgs{...})`. The
worker runs under `gor dev`; in production run `bin/{{.AppName}}-worker`
alongside the server.

## Email

```bash
gor g mailer welcome    # SendWelcome() + HTML template + Mailpit dev catcher
```

Send with `mailer.SendWelcome(ctx, to, mailer.WelcomeData{...})`. In dev,
Mailpit (in docker-compose) catches everything — nothing is really
delivered. Configure production SMTP via `SMTP_ADDR` etc. in the env.

## Realtime

```bash
gor g realtime    # SSE + Postgres NOTIFY — no broker
```

Publish with `realtime.Publish(ctx, "posts", payload)` (handlers or jobs);
subscribe with `useRealtimeInvalidate("posts", ["posts"])` for live lists,
or `useRealtime(channel, handler)` for raw events.

## Styling & theming

The frontend uses Tailwind CSS v4 with [shadcn/ui](https://ui.shadcn.com)
components (vendored under `web/src/components/ui/` — they're yours to edit).

- **Retheme the whole app in one place:** edit the CSS variables in
  `web/src/index.css` (`--primary`, `--radius`, ...). Every component reads
  those tokens, and a `.dark` palette is included (add `class="dark"` to
  `<html>` to use it).
- **Add more components:** `components.json` is set up, so from `web/`:

  ```bash
  npx shadcn@latest add dialog
  ```

## Project layout

```
cmd/server/        entrypoint, graceful shutdown
internal/
  config/          env config (.env in dev)
  db/              pgx pool; db/gen is sqlc output (don't edit)
  httpx/           JSON helpers, validation, error envelope
  middleware/      request logging, SPA serving
  routes/          route registration (gor appends here)
  <resource>/      one package per feature
migrations/        goose SQL migrations
queries/           sqlc query files (edit these, sqlc regenerates on save)
web/               React app; src/api = typed clients, src/features = pages
```

## Production

```bash
gor build          # builds web, embeds it, compiles bin/{{.AppName}}
APP_ENV=production DATABASE_URL=... ./bin/{{.AppName}}
```

One binary serves both the API and the frontend. For server deploys, build
a Docker image instead (multi-stage, distroless, ~27 MB):

```bash
gor build --image
docker run -p 8080:8080 -e DATABASE_URL=postgres://... {{.AppName}}:latest
```
